      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "Overweight and obesity are afflictions that lead to an increased risk of health problems including joint problems. The aim of the study was to assess the condition of articular cartilage in obese adolescent patients suffering from knee pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "MRI of 24 knees of 20 morbidly obese patients, mean age 14.2 years, was performed in an open 1.0 Tesla MR system, where the cartilage, the quality and structure of the menisci, and the presence or absence of surrounding changes was examined."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In all patients a cartilage lesion in at least one region of the knee could be detected. Retropatellar cartilage lesions have been found in 19 knees. Ten cartilage lesions grade I, and four lesions grade II have been described in the lateral compartment of the knee, whereas the medial compartment showed in eight cases a grade I, in 13 cases a grade II and in two cases a grade III cartilage lesion. Meniscal changes were assessed in most patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Morbidly obese children and adolescents show major abnormalities in the articular cartilage of the knee. Whether obesity alone is the causal factor for the development of the pattern of these changes, remains to be seen."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "KEY POINTS",
          "content": "\u2022 Morbidly obese children and adolescents often suffer from knee pain \u2022 Prospective study showed cartilage and meniscal lesions in morbidly obese adolescents \u2022 MRI is an adequate tool for assessing cartilage lesions even in morbidly obese patients \u2022 It is unclear whether cartilage abnormalities are mainly due to mechanical overload."
        }
      ]},
